\section*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Scope of the guarantee.} As with other distribution-free risk-control methods, the
guarantee holds when calibration and deployment tasks, and the model's outputs on them, share one
distribution, so thresholds should be calibrated on the target tasks and serving stack
(Appendix~\ref{app:sens}); recalibration takes under a second (Appendix~\ref{app:impl}). Weighted
or online calibration could extend it to shifting distributions. A served model behind a third-party
API, as Gemma is here, may change without notice, which this assumption does not cover.

\paragraph{Calibration data.} \climb{} uses every rung's outcome on a few hundred calibration
tasks, a one-off cost. With less data or tighter tolerances it conservatively falls back to the
full pipeline: at a 5-point tolerance it can certify less than an uncertified rule that happens to be
safe, which is the price of the guarantee. Stronger confidence signals would plug in directly.

\paragraph{Breadth of evaluation.} We evaluate three open models, one plan--execute--review pipeline
and four benchmarks. FRAMES and MuSiQue are given their gold documents rather than a retriever's, so
retrieval errors, which a team might catch or compound, are outside our measurements; Gemma's runs on
these and on BBEH were served locally and on MATH through a hosted provider, with the same checkpoint and
settings. On code generation, where a reviewer that cannot
run tests rarely improves the executor, the team helps little and escalation saves by staying cheap.
\climb{} needs only a cost-ordered ladder and a per-rung score, so other topologies require no change to
the calibration procedure. A few pipeline runs fill the generation cap on every call and end without an
answer (at most 0.5\% of tasks); they count as the pipeline's errors. Gemma's greedy direct answers and
some of its reviews fall into repetitions, which makes its confidence a misleading signal on three
benchmarks; we keep these behaviours, as a deployment would see them, rather than tune them away.
